  "summary": "Health spending surged in 2025 driven by expanded GLP-1 drug use and increased care volume, highlighting a major cost trend that will likely shape industry priorities. Meanwhile, AI continues to reshape healthcare delivery and drug development—from biotech companies increasingly integrating AI into research pipelines to health systems wrestling with AI governance challenges, with experts warning that successful implementation requires fixing foundational IT infrastructure first. Several women's health startups are emerging to address gaps the traditional healthcare system has left unmet, while clinical advances span from AI identifying sudden cardiac death risk to new gene therapy submissions for Duchenne muscular dystrophy and drug trials for an active Ebola outbreak. On the regulatory and business front, the FDA is moderating enforcement actions against certain digital health devices, venture funding remains robust across ophthalmology and chronic care automation, and healthcare organizations are grappling with cybersecurity incidents and consolidating legacy systems in preparation for digital transformation.",
